<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  what a run shows while it is not ready yet, drawn over the surface the guest will render into.

  **it is a screen rather than a widget, and that is the whole redesign.** a bar floating on the raw
  surface reads as something that has gone wrong on a black screen; a card on a filled background
  reads as the app, still working. it also solves a real problem the floating version had: the
  surface behind it is black until the guest draws, so text coloured for a light scheme was dark text
  on black. nothing here sits on the surface - the background is drawn.

  **and it is drawn black rather than in the scheme's own surface colour, which is the one place this
  screen does not follow the theme.** what is behind this view is the surface a guest is about to
  render into, which is black until it does, and what replaces this screen is a game's first frame,
  which is very often black as well. a field in the scheme's colour therefore steps to black at the
  exact moment the picture appears - the screen going away reads as a flash rather than as a card
  being lifted off. black on both sides of that moment means only the card disappears.

  the card is still the scheme's, which is what keeps this the app's screen rather than a blank one:
  the palette is carried by the thing being read, and the field around it is the dark the guest is
  about to fill. on this panel an unlit pixel costs nothing, which is the same reason the Dark theme
  is black rather than merely dark.

  **the card is a dialog, and it takes the dialog's own tokens rather than numbers copied off one.**
  `MaterialAlertDialog` carries no style override in this app, so every popup here is Material3's -
  filled with colorSurfaceContainerHigh, cornered by shapeAppearanceCornerExtraLarge, padded 24dp at
  the sides, and with no outline at all. naming those two attributes is what keeps this the same
  shape as the FEXCore preset dialog on a day the library moves them.

  it is not a real dialog because there is no theme to host one: a dialog is a window, and this
  activity's window is the surface a guest renders into. so it is the dialog's clothes on a card.

  **it is laid out for a wait of several seconds, not for the quarter of a second the bundled build
  takes.** that is deliberate: what is on screen has to be worth reading, because the thing that uses
  it most is a game boot. so there is the game's own cover and name, a line saying what is happening
  to it right now, a bar that moves, and a figure that says how far along it is.

  **the heading is the game and the line under it is the phase**, which is the way round that keeps
  the screen still. the phase changes ten times in seven seconds; a heading that changed with it
  would be the most restless thing on a screen whose whole job is to be reassuring. what a person
  wants confirmed first is that the thing they tapped is the thing that is loading.

  **there is no separate screen for unpacking the copy of SharpEmu that ships in the app.** it is one
  more phase on this one, named by the same line - it happens once per install, immediately before a
  boot, and a screen of its own would mean two waits dressed differently inside one launch.

  ConstraintLayout for exactly one property: `layout_constraintWidth_max`. the card wants to be a
  readable column rather than the width of a landscape panel, and a fixed width would be wrong on a
  device of another shape - no other container here can say "as wide as there is room for, up to
  this".

  there is no `-land` variant and there is not meant to be. the activity is pinned landscape - see
  the manifest - so a second composition would be one nothing can reach.
-->
<androidx.constraintlayout.widget.ConstraintLayout
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:background="@android:color/black">

    <com.google.android.material.card.MaterialCardView
        android:layout_width="0dp"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginHorizontal="24dp"
        app:cardBackgroundColor="?attr/colorSurfaceContainerHigh"
        app:cardElevation="0dp"
        app:layout_constraintBottom_toBottomOf="parent"
        app:layout_constraintEnd_toEndOf="parent"
        app:layout_constraintStart_toStartOf="parent"
        app:layout_constraintTop_toTopOf="parent"
        app:layout_constraintWidth_max="440dp"
        app:shapeAppearance="?attr/shapeAppearanceCornerExtraLarge"
        app:strokeWidth="0dp">

        <!-- **the sides are the dialog's 24dp and the ends are not, and that is the title's doing.**
             a dialog pays 24dp above a title whose type sits tight in its box; this heading is a
             textAppearance with its own leading above the caps, so the same number draws visibly more
             air. 16dp here is what lands the first line where a dialog's title lands. the bottom is
             short of 24dp for the mirror of that reason - the last line is a figure, which has
             descender space under it that nothing occupies. -->
        <LinearLayout
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:orientation="vertical"
            android:paddingHorizontal="24dp"
            android:paddingTop="16dp"
            android:paddingBottom="18dp">

            <!-- the cover and the two lines beside it. **a row rather than the cover above them**,
                 because the bar underneath wants the card's full width and a centred composition
                 would leave it the only thing not centred. the artwork also has to be able to be
                 absent - a dump carrying no sce_sys/ is a game that boots perfectly well - and a row
                 closes up around a missing cover where a column would leave a hole. -->
            <LinearLayout
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:gravity="center_vertical"
                android:orientation="horizontal">

                <!-- the same drawing the game list draws, at the same ratio and a smaller echo of
                     the same radius - see item_game.xml, whose shape overlay this is. it is GONE
                     rather than the placeholder when a launch named no artwork, because `am start`
                     names none and a grey placeholder in a popup reads as a failed load rather than
                     as a launch that was never told. -->
                <com.google.android.material.imageview.ShapeableImageView
                    android:id="@+id/loading_cover"
                    android:layout_width="72dp"
                    android:layout_height="72dp"
                    android:layout_marginEnd="16dp"
                    android:importantForAccessibility="no"
                    android:scaleType="centerCrop"
                    android:visibility="gone"
                    app:shapeAppearanceOverlay="@style/ShapeAppearance.SharpEmu.Tile"
                    tools:src="@drawable/ic_game_placeholder"
                    tools:visibility="visible" />

                <LinearLayout
                    android:layout_width="0dp"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_weight="1"
                    android:orientation="vertical">

                    <!-- two lines and then ellipsized, not the marquee the game list's cards use. a
                         card in a grid is one line tall and every card has to be the same height, so
                         a long name there has nowhere to go but sideways; here there is room for a
                         second line, and a name sliding under a phase line that is also changing
                         would be two moving things in a block of four. -->
                    <TextView
                        android:id="@+id/loading_title"
                        android:layout_width="match_parent"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:ellipsize="end"
                        android:maxLines="2"
                        android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceTitleLarge"
                        android:textColor="?attr/colorOnSurface"
                        tools:text="Dreaming Sarah" />

                    <!-- what is happening to it right now. **it is one line and it is held to one**,
                         because every phase name is short and a line that reflowed to two would move
                         the bar under it a dozen times in a boot. -->
                    <TextView
                        android:id="@+id/loading_detail"
                        android:layout_width="match_parent"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:layout_marginTop="4dp"
                        android:ellipsize="end"
                        android:singleLine="true"
                        android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceBodyMedium"
                        android:textColor="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant"
                        tools:text="Preparing the display…" />

                </LinearLayout>

            </LinearLayout>

            <!-- **the track is the card's own outline colour**, which is what keeps an empty bar
                 reading as a groove in the card rather than as a second surface on it. every scheme
                 names that role, which the surfaceContainer* family above outlineVariant does not -
                 an unnamed role is Material3's baseline lilac, and this app is three quarters
                 monochrome.

                 **the range is a thousand rather than a hundred**, and that is not precision for its
                 own sake: the boot bar is redrawn on every animation frame from a continuous
                 estimate, so a hundred steps over seven seconds is a visible tick every 70 ms on a
                 bar nearly 400dp wide. the figure below is still a whole percent - the resolution is
                 the sweep's, not the label's. -->
            <!-- **the stop indicator is off, and it is a feature rather than an accident.** Material
                 draws a dot at the end of the track, in the indicator's own colour, to mark where a
                 determinate bar is heading. on a bar that sweeps continuously for several seconds it
                 reads instead as the leading edge of the line, detached and already arrived - and it
                 marks a target that is already stated twice over, by the figure under the bar and by
                 the track simply ending. so it costs a misreading and buys nothing here.

                 it is also drawn in determinate mode alone, so leaving it on would make this screen
                 structurally different with the estimate on and off for a reason that has nothing to
                 do with the estimate. -->
            <com.google.android.material.progressindicator.LinearProgressIndicator
                android:id="@+id/loading_bar"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginTop="22dp"
                android:max="1000"
                app:indicatorColor="?attr/colorPrimary"
                app:trackColor="?attr/colorOutlineVariant"
                app:trackCornerRadius="4dp"
                app:trackStopIndicatorSize="0dp"
                app:trackThickness="8dp" />

            <!-- **under the bar and at its end, which is where the bar is going.** over it, the
                 figure competes with the heading; at the start, the eye leaves it before the bar has
                 said anything. it is gone entirely when there is no figure to give, so an
                 indeterminate wait does not draw an empty line. -->
            <TextView
                android:id="@+id/loading_percent"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginTop="10dp"
                android:gravity="end"
                android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceLabelLarge"
                android:textColor="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant"
                tools:text="38%" />

        </LinearLayout>

    </com.google.android.material.card.MaterialCardView>

</androidx.constraintlayout.widget.ConstraintLayout>
